  "content": {
    "id": "p20-q19-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "19",
    "prompt": "Suppose $\\triangle ABC$ is right-angled with side lengths $3$, $4$ and $5$. Denoting the right angle by $\\angle C$, find the length of the line segment from $C$ to $AB$ that bisects $\\angle C$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{12\\sqrt2}{7}$.\n\nUse the Angle Bisector Theorem, then Pythagoras in a $45^\\circ$ right triangle.",
      "worked": "Let the bisector meet $AB$ at $D$. Take $AC=3$ and $BC=4$; interchanging these gives the same result. The Angle Bisector Theorem gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{AD}{DB}&=\\frac34,\\\\AD&=\\frac37\\times5=\\frac{15}{7}.\\end{align*}$$\nDraw $DH\\perp AC$. Since $\\angle DCH=45^\\circ$, $CH=DH$. Also $\\triangle AHD\\sim\\triangle ACB$ by AAA, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{DH}{BC}&=\\frac{AD}{AB},\\\\DH&=4\\times\\frac{15/7}{5}=\\frac{12}{7},\\\\CD^2&=CH^2+DH^2,\\\\CD^2&=2\\left(\\frac{12}{7}\\right)^2,\\\\CD&=\\frac{12\\sqrt2}{7}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 90
  },
